Z||000000||opm2000||03-13-2001||03:09 PM||||Can anyone provide a phone number, web address, or contact person for Fiero Specialties, in Florida?<P>I assume they are still in business, but who knows?<P>David Breeze||||reg||5
Z||000001||rockcrawl||03-13-2001||07:17 PM||||If you're looking for info on the longitudinal V8 swap, it's Greg at Fiero Concepts, 100 W. Wonder St. Wildwood, FL 34785 (352)-748-7033. I haven't talked to him for a few years. I need to update my website now that I've found that info again.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000002||opm2000||03-13-2001||08:53 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR>[B]If you're looking for info on the longitudinal V8 swap...<P>Hi Jon,<BR>         Yep, today I purchased a TH325 from a '79 Buick Riveria.   I don't pick it up untill this thursday.   You mentioned that Greg might be a source of further information.<P>   I'm really interested in the reverse mounting concept.   If anyone has further information or findings reguarding this method, I'd sure appreciate hearing about it.<P>   I haven't begun the frame cutting and fabricating phase yet, so either installation is ok, I guess.   And since I haven't actually seen this transmission yet, I'm having a bit of a hard time envisioning just what is required.<P>   As far as setting the frame cuts & fabricating for a non-reversed setup like you did, I think I can go on what you now have on your website.<P>Anyhow, this is being mated to a '94 LT1 and put into a Diablo GT rebody.<P>More to come.<BR>David Breeze <P>||||reg||10
Z||000003||rockcrawl||03-13-2001||09:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Anyhow, this is being mated to a '94 LT1 and put into a Diablo GT rebody<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Oooooh Baby, that should scream! Once you get the trans, you'll see how the differential housing bolts on, and how it can be flipped upside down to make it run backwards which also effectively lowers the engine by putting the axle centerline above the pinion centerline. So there is really more than one advantage to running it backwards. I started mine before I talked to Greg, but he's the one who told me in about doing it backwards. Plus it sure would be neat to have the pulleys and belts facing the rear just like a real Lambo!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||onfire||03-14-2001||01:11 PM||||Have you guys seen this guys Diablo. Not exactly what your looking for on the engine, but it may have other info that interests you. <A HREF="http://www.lambobuilder.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lambobuilder.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||03-14-2001||06:16 PM||||Yes, I've talked to Dale before. He shared some info about power steering.||||reg||1
Z||000006||opm2000||03-14-2001||09:46 PM||||Dale's site led me to two other builders who used the '85 Chevette power steering setup.   Since this rebody is for my wife, we are exploring that install, also.  Carparts lists a new unit for @ $175 or so.<P>BTW, I tried the phone number for Gary in Florida, and got a disconnected message.   Man, I'd really like to talk with someone who has actually done the reverse/rotate with the TH425/TH325.<P>Otherwise, I may have to drop in for a visit with you this spring, John !  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||14
